# results/raw/ IS COMMITTED, as of 2026-08-16.
#
# It used to be ignored here, with the reason "regenerable from the pod, too big for
# git". Both halves were wrong. It is 13 MB of CSV across 62 files — not large. And it
# is NOT regenerable: it is the output of 31 models on rented GPUs, one of which
# (greedy decoding) is measurably non-deterministic across runs, so re-collecting it
# would produce different numbers rather than the same ones.
#
# The practical consequence, found by cloning the repo and running the documented
# pipeline: `build_analysis_data.py` failed immediately on a fresh clone, so the
# raw -> derived step of a project whose entire subject is measurement provenance
# could not be reproduced by anyone but its author.
#
# Model WEIGHTS are excluded below and always will be.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
env/
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Model weights — never commit these
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.gguf

# Built presentation and blog post — COMMITTED, as of 2026-08-16.
#
# This rule used to read `*.pptx`, on the reasoning that the deck is regenerable from
# `scripts/make_deck.py` plus `figures/`, so committing the binary could only let it
# drift from its source or bloat the history.
#
# Reversed deliberately. The drift half of that argument was the weaker one: both
# binaries are now written by a generator that re-derives or asserts every number it
# prints (`make_deck.py --check`, `make_blog.py --check`), and the generators are
# committed beside them. The bloat half is real and accepted, at roughly 1.6 MB per deck
# rebuild, because the artifacts are the deliverable and a reader who wants the talk or
# the write-up should not have to clone the repo and install matplotlib to see it.
#
# THE STANDING RULE IS UNCHANGED and is the one that matters: a committed artifact must
# reproduce from a committed generator. Regenerate before committing, never hand-edit
# the binary. Neither .docx nor .pptx is byte-reproducible (both are zips carrying
# timestamps), so `test_artifacts_reproduce.py` cannot cover them the way it covers
# results/derived/*.md. The `--check` modes are the substitute, and they are weaker.

# CI scratch: the captured pytest run that the workflow turns into an annotation.
pytest-out.txt

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/

# Secrets — nothing here should ever be committed
.env
*.pem
*_token*
!scripts/*token*

# Personal Claude Code permission grants — machine-specific, not part of the project
.claude/settings.local.json
